ets>: net '/IN' nodes J1 pin 1 + R1 pin 1; net '/OUT' nodes C1 pin 1, J1 pin 2, R1 pin 2; net 'GND' nodes C1 pin 2, J1 pin 3\"},{\"path\":\"work/netlist/connectivity.json\",\"locator\":\"nets: /IN=[J1.1,R1.1], /OUT=[C1.1,J1.2,R1.2], GND=[C1.2,J1.3]\"},{\"path\":\"work/geometry/geometry.json\",\"locator\":\"J1 pads: pad 1 net /IN at (100,100), pad 2 net /OUT at (100,102.54), pad 3 net GND at (100,105.08)\"},{\"path\":\"review-work/physical/experiments/notes.md\",\"locator\":\"sections 1-3: independent netlist re-export, pin-position derivation, pcbnew pad/track dump (unconnected=0)\"},{\"path\":\"review-work/physical/experiments/my_netlist.xml\",\"locator\":\"<nets> block — independently regenerated, identical to packet netlist\"}],\"reasoning\":\"Three independent derivations agree: (1) geometric reconstruction of J1's pin positions under its saved 180-degree rotation maps pin 1 to the wire labeled 'IN' and pin 2 to 'OUT'; (2) a fresh kicad-cli netlist export reproduces /IN=[J1.1,R1.1] and /OUT=[C1.1,J1.2,R1.2]; (3) pcbnew reads the same pad-net assignments from the board, and the copper implements them. Electrically the net on J1.1 is unambiguously the filter input (it reaches only the free end of series R1) and the net on J1.2 is the filtered R-C node, so this is a true role swap at the connector, not a naming quibble. It directly violates the only evaluated criterion, which supports blocking severity; schematic and board agree with each other, so the fix is a design change (reassign J1 pins 1/2), not a layout parity repair.\"},{\"id\":\"PHY-002\",\"title\":\"Criteria set cannot detect the connector-contract violation: all native checks pass on an internally consistent but contract-swapped design\",\"location\":\"packet checks 'erc', 'drc', 'netlist', 'geometry'; work/erc/erc.json, work/drc/drc.json\",\"condition\":\"ERC reports 0 violations, DRC with --schematic-parity reports 0 violations/0 unconnected/0 parity issues, and the netlist/geometry probes are info-only. None of these checks encodes the external requirement that net roles map to specific J1 pin numbers, so the suite returns clean results on a design whose sole acceptance criterion fails (PHY-001).\",\"mechanism\":\"ERC/DRC/parity verify internal self-consistency (schematic vs board, copper rules), not requirement-level pin assignments. A pin-role swap propagated consistently through schematic and layout produces zero findings; a successful tool exit is then mistaken for contract compliance unless a dedicated assertion (e.g. expected net name per J1 pad number) is run against the netlist or geometry output.\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"assessment\":\"concern\",\"evidence\":[{\"path\":\"work/erc/console.log\",\"locator\":\"'Found 0 violations'\"},{\"path\":\"work/drc/console.log\",\"locator\":\"'Found 0 violations / 0 unconnected items / 0 schematic parity issues'\"},{\"path\":\"work/netlist/design.xml\",\"locator\":\"<nets> block shows J1.1=/IN, J1.2=/OUT — data sufficient to detect the swap, but no check in the packet asserts on it\"},{\"path\":\"review-work/physical/experiments/notes.md\",\"locator\":\"Conclusion section: self-consistency masks contract violation from ERC/DRC/parity\"}],\"reasoning\":\"This is a verification-coverage observation, not a tool failure: the tools ran correctly and their outputs are reproducible (I re-ran the netlist export with identical results). The gap is that the declared criteria (erc, drc, netlist, geometry) contain no assertion binding net /IN to J1 pad 2 and /OUT to J1 pad 1 per the contract, so green results provide no evidence for the one requirement under review. Flagged as a concern so the harness adds a pin-role assertion; severity minor because the data needed for such a check is already produced by the existing netlist/geometry evidence.\"}]}","structured_output":{"candidate_id":"21b0c14a5c2860d4ccecc4fe1758409cf93d368265316a1c5f42d3577cedea7e","run_id":"7c0edd5c-e4f4-4823-90d9-cdd624e68f5e","packet_sha256":"6d516b200c